requirements:
Tubes must be manufactured from one of seven specified grades of copper or copper alloy designed for particular thermal, mechanical, and corrosion-resistance conditions:
a) Cu-DLP: Phosphorized Low Residual Phosphorus Copper (Purity: >=99.90% min copper, P: 0.004-0.012%);
b) Cu-DHP: Phosphorized High Residual Phosphorus Copper (Purity: >=99.90% min copper, P: 0.015-0.040%);
b) CuZn30As: 70/30 Brass (Arsenical, for bio-fouling and dezincification resistance);
d) CuZn29Sn1As: Admiralty Brass (Arsenical, typical for freshwater/saline condensers);
e) CuZn21Al2As: Aluminium Brass (Arsenical, typical for high-velocity cooling water);
f) CuNi10Fe1: Cupronickel 90/10 (corrosion and erosion resistant);
g) CuNi30Mn1Fe: Cupronickel 70/30 (highly resistant to seawater and high-temperature steam).

Tubes must be supplied in one of five distinct physical conditions as specified by the purchaser:
a) As Drawn (HD): Full hard condition produced by cold drawing, which can be stress-relieved;
b) As Drawn and End Annealed: Full hard condition but with tube ends fully annealed to prevent cracking during expansion into tube-sheets;
c) Half Hard (HB): Produced by cold drawing to an intermediate hardness;
d) Temper Annealed (TA): Heat-treated over their whole length to an intermediate condition;
e) Annealed (O): Fully annealed for maximum ductility and malleability.

Strict geometric definitions are established to ensure leak-tight fittings and uniform flow:
a) Eccentricity: calculated as (t_max - t_min) / t_avg where t is the wall thickness;
b) Ovality: calculated as (OD_max - OD_min) / OD_avg where OD is the outside diameter;
c) Out of Roundness: defined as the absolute difference (OD_max - OD_min).
